00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:55.970
And the handles actually did get very hot.

533
00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:58.250
You know, nowadays they'd probably do CGI

534
00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:02.030
and maybe even make the door handle a little red with the CGI.

535
00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:05.230
But that was all completely real.

536
00:23:06.170 --> 00:23:08.490
I can't believe they gave him a real blowtorch.

537
00:23:08.610 --> 00:23:14.930
I mean, if you look back over the things they gave Rainn that were real in episodes.

538
00:23:15.070 --> 00:23:19.950
They should have known going back to the Dundies when they gave him that operating keyboard.

539
00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:22.910
Like just what he did with the fart key.

540
00:23:24.230 --> 00:23:25.770
Don't give that guy real things.

541
00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:26.790
Don't give that guy real things.

542
00:23:27.010 --> 00:23:27.670
Well, yeah.

543
00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:29.230
So real blowtorch, what else?

544
00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:31.270
Well, we had another fan question.

545
00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:34.370
People wanted to know if Rainn was really smoking.

546
00:23:35.270 --> 00:23:39.690
And Rohit R wanted to know if it was real smoke in the trash can.

547
00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:41.570
Here's what Rainn had to say.

548
00:23:41.570 --> 00:23:48.390
It was an actual cigarette that I smoked and lit and I threw it in the garbage can with the paper.

549
00:23:48.550 --> 00:23:54.310
But I think they like ran in the second the camera panned away and tossed in some water and put it out.

550
00:23:54.470 --> 00:24:01.090
And the fire that you see in the trash can is expertly created due to the magic of special effects.

551
00:24:01.090 --> 00:24:07.910
There are so many amazing details throughout the episode that Paul Lieberstein, I remember guiding me through.

552
00:24:08.130 --> 00:24:09.090
I mean, I think he wrote it.

553
00:24:09.190 --> 00:24:16.410
But Paul was very instrumental in a lot of the details about how I smoked and how I casually tossed the cigarette.

554
00:24:16.410 --> 00:24:24.770
And he had it kind of visualized in his mind how the wedges get hammered under the door and all the details, the hammer in my pocket and stuff like that.

555
00:24:24.770 --> 00:24:26.790
So the great Paul Lieberstein, a.k.a.

556
00:24:26.890 --> 00:24:34.470
Toby, really played a great creative part throughout the entirety of the office and especially in this episode in this cold open.

557
00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:35.790
Wow.

558
00:24:35.870 --> 00:24:36.650
I didn't know that.

559
00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:42.630
I didn't know Paul was behind all those beats, all the, like, staging of the fire moments.

560
00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:43.870
Yeah, me either.

561
00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:50.170
Also, Jenna, just on a side note, I've never had to smoke a cigarette in a movie or TV show.

562
00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:55.190
And it would be a disaster because I don't know how to smoke a cigarette and it would be so clear.

563
00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:57.450
I wouldn't know how to be, like, fakie smoky.

564
00:24:57.770 --> 00:25:06.630
Well, for all of our, like, kind of techie nerds out there, Randy broke down for me exactly how they did make that fire in the trash can, that smoke.

565
00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:08.410
So Rain was right, right?

566
00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:10.430
They extinguished the fire that you saw.

567
00:25:10.550 --> 00:25:17.830
But then to create that smoke billowing in, that was created by a special effects team led by Ron Nary.

568
00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:26.930
And for the smoke, he used a water vapor generator with a minute concentration of glycol.

569
00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:32.670
And I guess the glycol is what keeps the water vapor hanging in the air a bit longer.

570
00:25:33.170 --> 00:25:39.510
Randy said it had no odor and it was approved safe by both SAG and NBC Universal Safety Department.

571
00:25:39.810 --> 00:25:41.250
What is glycol?

572
00:25:41.770 --> 00:25:42.630
I don't know.

573
00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:45.090
Google it?

574
00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:46.470
You can Google it.

575
00:25:46.550 --> 00:25:47.270
You can Google it.

576
00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:48.890
What is it?

577
00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:50.830
Is it glycol?

578
00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:52.910
Okay.

579
00:25:53.150 --> 00:25:54.390
There's two glycols.

580
00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:55.190
Oh.

581
00:25:56.530 --> 00:25:58.290
There's propylene glycol.

582
00:25:58.630 --> 00:25:58.930
Okay.

583
00:25:59.270 --> 00:26:01.910
And then there's ethylene glycol.

584
00:26:02.150 --> 00:26:04.090
Was one a good guy and one's not great?

585
00:26:04.310 --> 00:26:05.250
I guess kind of.

586
00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:09.890
I think the ethylene glycol is the stuff that's in, like, antifreeze.

587
00:26:10.030 --> 00:26:10.290
Oh.

588
00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:11.690
And it has, like, a sweet taste.

589
00:26:11.830 --> 00:26:13.330
That's what, it's poisonous, right?

590
00:26:13.470 --> 00:26:13.520
To animals.

591
00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:14.910
You don't want your dog to lick it.

592
00:26:15.130 --> 00:26:15.270
Right.

593
00:26:15.390 --> 00:26:17.450
They're always saying, don't lick your antifreeze.

594
00:26:17.830 --> 00:26:19.010
They're always saying that.

595
00:26:19.130 --> 00:26:19.750
To dogs.

596
00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:22.730
The dogs are like, we don't understand you.

597
00:26:22.830 --> 00:26:23.950
No, cats love it, too.

598
00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:24.790
You got to be careful.

599
00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:30.730
Now, propylene glycol is a substance that's commonly used as a food additive.

600
00:26:31.330 --> 00:26:35.690
It's in a lot of cosmetic and hygiene products, including, I think, some toothpastes.

601
00:26:36.330 --> 00:26:36.530
Oh.

602
00:26:36.710 --> 00:26:38.090
So that's the one we used.

603
00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:39.550
I mean, I guess so.

604
00:26:39.790 --> 00:26:40.830
Well, I think.

605
00:26:41.170 --> 00:26:45.570
One of the glycols can also be used as some sort of a fiber.

606
00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:51.130
I don't think we have time to, in the moment, deep dive glycol, but we were curious.

607
00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:57.930
So I'm assuming that since SAG and NBCUniversal said it was safe, then we used the safe one.

608
00:26:58.050 --> 00:26:59.150
We used the safe one.

609
00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:00.270
Look at us.

610
00:27:00.370 --> 00:27:00.870
We're fine.

611
00:27:01.870 --> 00:27:02.770
We're here.

612
00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:06.570
Next fan question is from Mikey S.

613
00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:11.710
Did Steve improvise when he said, stay effing calm?

614
00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:13.050
No.

615
00:27:13.570 --> 00:27:14.790
A little bit, though.

616
00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:16.530
Well, that's Steve, right?

617
00:27:16.630 --> 00:27:20.350
I mean, he got everything scripted, but sometimes he would zhuzh in the moment.

618
00:27:20.710 --> 00:27:22.310
Here's what it said in the script.

619
00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:23.690
Oh, God.

620
00:27:23.890 --> 00:27:24.630
It's happening.

621
00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:25.690
Let me through.

622
00:27:25.930 --> 00:27:26.650
Let me through.

623
00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:27.790
Stay calm.

624
00:27:27.970 --> 00:27:28.630
Stay calm.

625
00:27:28.810 --> 00:27:30.250
Stay effing calm.

626
00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:34.690
And then Michael violently pushes his way through to the front.

627
00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:37.190
I think we did it.

628
00:27:37.510 --> 00:27:39.330
I mean, very accurately.

